WEBVTT

00:00:16.250 --> 00:00:18.910
H-Hey, where are you going?! In you go!

00:00:18.910 --> 00:00:20.200
Oh, do be quiet.

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:20.910
Wha--?

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:25.120
What is it, Holmes? Did you find something else?

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.700
Yeah.

00:00:27.870 --> 00:00:29.660
This is interesting.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:34.040
Well then...

00:00:34.040 --> 00:00:36.410
Lestrade...

00:00:36.410 --> 00:00:38.200
...let me escape.

00:00:51.160 --> 00:00:54.000
<i>The mist is hiding the sin</i>

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:56.750
<i>I hold in my hand the LIST of judgment</i>

00:00:56.750 --> 00:00:59.370
<i>I'm gonna do the perfect crime</i>
<i>and bury them in a cist</i>

00:00:59.370 --> 00:01:04.620
<i>That's my DYING WISH for you</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:10.040
<i>Guys of the privileged class torment the weak</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:10.040 --> 00:01:13.120
<i>And they enjoy it</i>

00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:18.700
<i>That's a bloody world, an endlessly</i>
<i>discriminatory society (dystopia)</i>

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:23.700
<i>Happiness does not bloom here</i>
<i>(Set a thief to catch another thief)</i>

00:01:23.700 --> 00:01:26.830
<i>We are oppressed</i>

00:01:26.830 --> 00:01:32.410
<i>We must do something. Come on</i>

00:01:32.410 --> 00:01:38.870
<i>Let the end begin</i>

00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:44.700
<i>Break it to relieve your soul</i>

00:01:44.700 --> 00:01:50.620
<i>Open your eyes. You'll be reborn</i>
<i>when you lose your life</i>

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:55.700
<i>We're lords of crime. We're sacrifice for ideals</i>

00:01:55.700 --> 00:02:08.000
<i>Freedom lives forever, even if we die</i>

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:14.750
"A Study in 'S' Act 2"

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.160
"A Study in 'S' Act 2"

00:02:14.750 --> 00:02:18.160
A consulting detective, indeed!

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:18.950
"A Study in 'S' Act 2"

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:23.200
He's finished this time, Inspector.

00:02:27.040 --> 00:02:30.290
Lestrade, let me escape.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.250
All I want to do is solve the mystery.

00:02:34.250 --> 00:02:38.790
When the true culprit is caught,
you can have all the credit.

00:02:38.790 --> 00:02:42.120
So I need you to do a little job for me.

00:02:44.200 --> 00:02:50.330
The true culprit, eh? Are you sure
you can find them, Holmes?

00:02:53.910 --> 00:02:54.870
Ah! Hold it!

00:02:54.870 --> 00:02:56.870
Oi!

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.580
Holmes!

00:02:58.580 --> 00:02:59.450
Inspector!

00:02:59.450 --> 00:03:02.790
S-Sorry! I lost control of
the horse all of a sudden.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:13.540
So sorry to keep you waiting.

00:03:16.250 --> 00:03:17.750
Sherlock!

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:23.120
I'm so glad you managed to get away.

00:03:25.290 --> 00:03:30.250
The inspector cooperated with you,
just like you wrote in your note.

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:35.080
Yeah. But now, I'm every bit a wanted man.

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:38.410
John, are you sure you want to help me?

00:03:38.410 --> 00:03:44.410
Of course. After all, if you're caught,
I'll have to pay the entire rent by myself.

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:48.700
Then we'll have to settle this right away,
to ensure that doesn't happen.

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:51.620
Wait, does that mean you already
know who the true culprit is?

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.580
I don't know <i>who </i>it is just yet.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:59.370
However, all we need to do is set
a trap using this to lure them in.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.200
A ring?

00:04:02.450 --> 00:04:05.660
On that occasion, did you...?

00:04:05.660 --> 00:04:09.080
I seem to have a talent for pilfering things.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:15.370
I only pray that going forward,
you use your abilities for good.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:19.500
You lost your ring at the scene?

00:04:19.500 --> 00:04:21.500
I'm sorry.

00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:25.160
But I need to get it back, whatever it takes.

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.950
It's more precious to me than my life!

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:31.540
Are you all right?

00:04:39.160 --> 00:04:46.290
"I found a golden ring in 3 Lauriston Gardens,
off the Brixton Road.

00:04:46.290 --> 00:04:53.700
If you might be the owner, please contact me.
221B Baker Street, Dr John H. Watson."

00:04:54.870 --> 00:04:57.370
So he's setting a trap.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.160
Are you sure we should send Fred?

00:05:02.160 --> 00:05:05.250
Improvisation is a part of theatre.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:08.910
Moreover, this is Hope's final wish.

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.790
I see no harm in granting it.

00:05:12.790 --> 00:05:16.080
Good. The carrot has been dangled.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:18.120
What happens now?

00:05:18.120 --> 00:05:19.750
Oh, just watch.

00:05:19.750 --> 00:05:22.790
Holmes, I've rounded everybody up.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:24.410
Who are you?

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:29.620
I'm Wiggins, captain of
the Baker Street Irregulars.

00:05:29.620 --> 00:05:33.080
Baker Street Irregulars?

00:05:33.080 --> 00:05:37.000
As you can see, a band
of homeless, grimy urchins.

00:05:37.000 --> 00:05:39.120
You didn't have to call us grimy.

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:42.870
They do it all, from finding
lost items to running errands.

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:48.750
After all, the whole city is their home.
They're far more helpful than the police.

00:05:48.750 --> 00:05:52.290
They're sort of like my eyes and ears.

00:05:52.290 --> 00:05:57.500
Don't treat us like we're your minions.
There's no way we work for free!

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:02.080
All right, all right. You'll get
your reward, just do your job.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.290
Sherlock, what are you having them do for you?

00:06:06.290 --> 00:06:08.830
Just providing a little assistance.

00:06:14.620 --> 00:06:19.160
Lucy, eh? This is a women's wedding band.

00:06:19.160 --> 00:06:23.290
Judging by the design, it's not an aristocrat's.

00:06:23.290 --> 00:06:27.410
By Sherlock's deduction,
the culprit dropped this.

00:06:27.410 --> 00:06:30.290
Meaning that it's our one piece of evidence.

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:35.870
If it belongs to the killer, they are
certain to come and take it back.

00:06:35.870 --> 00:06:38.750
Given the width of his finger,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:43.160
the culprit is a man much larger
than our diminutive count.

00:06:43.160 --> 00:06:46.580
The killer is a large man...

00:06:55.500 --> 00:06:58.870
Yes, who is it?

00:06:58.870 --> 00:07:03.870
I'm here for the ring you
posted about in the newspaper.

00:07:03.870 --> 00:07:08.700
It belongs to my daughter, Lucy.

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.330
An old woman?

00:07:10.330 --> 00:07:14.370
The ring has her name
inscribed on the inside, yes?

00:07:14.370 --> 00:07:16.000
Y-Yes.

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:20.370
My daughter says that she dropped it
somewhere near Number 3.

00:07:20.370 --> 00:07:23.910
She just got married last year.

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:27.000
If her husband finds out
she lost her precious ring,

00:07:27.000 --> 00:07:30.620
he would be terribly angry with her.

00:07:33.870 --> 00:07:37.450
I see. Well then, here you are.

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:14.750
Quite the spry little old lady, isn't she?!

00:08:50.000 --> 00:08:53.410
Now, to find out who you really are--

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:57.250
Sherlock!

00:09:08.950 --> 00:09:13.370
Damn! I guess they aren't about to
let themselves be caught so easily.

00:09:13.370 --> 00:09:15.700
They really got one over on me.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.910
Was that man just now the formidable
mastermind that you mentioned?

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:25.290
No, he's probably one of the mastermind's men.

00:09:25.290 --> 00:09:31.410
One of the mastermind's men?
So then, they're a sort of organisation?

00:09:31.410 --> 00:09:38.080
Most likely. But right now,
finding the count's killer comes first.

00:09:38.080 --> 00:09:42.040
But they took the ring that was our only lead.

00:09:42.040 --> 00:09:43.160
Huh?

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:49.200
What are you talking about, John?
The murder is all but solved at this point.

00:09:49.200 --> 00:09:52.250
You know who actually killed the count?!

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:53.290
Yeah.

00:09:53.290 --> 00:09:56.580
Then who on earth was it?

00:09:56.580 --> 00:10:02.410
This worked out just right.
Wait for me over at that shop.

00:10:02.410 --> 00:10:04.080
I'll be right back.

00:10:04.080 --> 00:10:06.120
H-Hey!

00:10:06.120 --> 00:10:08.120
Sherlock!

00:10:18.540 --> 00:10:23.540
Has Sherlock really discovered
who the killer is?

00:10:23.540 --> 00:10:26.540
I wish he would have told me right away.

00:10:28.910 --> 00:10:31.250
Let's go, John.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:38.660
This arrived from Lestrade. Read through it.

00:10:39.910 --> 00:10:45.040
I asked Lestrade to check
into Count Drebber's past.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:48.200
The man was an incomparable philanderer,

00:10:48.200 --> 00:10:52.500
and had a particular fondness for
molesting women of lower standing.

00:10:52.500 --> 00:10:55.700
A number of them apparently had
some considerably awful experiences.

00:10:55.700 --> 00:10:59.870
It seems he usually settled
those matters with his money.

00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:04.290
But there was one amongst them who
proved to be an exception to that rule.

00:11:04.290 --> 00:11:05.870
Lucy Ferrier.

00:11:05.870 --> 00:11:11.580
She exchanged wedding vows
with Jefferson Hope, a local farmer.

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:16.250
But after she went missing,
her remains did come back to him.

00:11:16.250 --> 00:11:22.410
There were reportedly a fair number
of horrifying bruises on her body.

00:11:26.250 --> 00:11:29.500
That is a positively tragic story.

00:11:29.500 --> 00:11:32.120
So in that case, the true killer is...

00:11:32.120 --> 00:11:38.450
Yeah, the same Jefferson Hope is the man
who killed Count Drebber, as well as...

00:11:38.450 --> 00:11:41.950
...the driver of our cab.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:45.200
Bingo, right...

00:11:45.200 --> 00:11:46.830
...Hope?

00:11:48.950 --> 00:11:53.950
You surprise me. I did not expect you
to investigate so thoroughly.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:58.580
John, riding in a carriage driven by a murderer
isn't something you often get to experience.

00:11:58.580 --> 00:12:04.160
H-Hold on! Even if the name "Lucy" on
the ring is connected to Jefferson Hope,

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.540
that alone wouldn't mean that our driver
is this same Hope, would it?

00:12:08.540 --> 00:12:10.450
No, you're right.

00:12:10.450 --> 00:12:14.250
I came at this from two
different lines of reasoning.

00:12:14.250 --> 00:12:18.080
One was the ring you just mentioned.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:21.120
The other one was some wheel tracks.

00:12:21.120 --> 00:12:25.950
Thanks to some rain that evening at
the scene, the tracks were quite clear.

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:28.830
All of the houses on that street
belonged to noblemen,

00:12:28.830 --> 00:12:32.290
who own four-wheeled, two-horse carriages.

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:39.500
However, in front of the count's house alone,
there were tracks from a smaller, narrow cab.

00:12:40.580 --> 00:12:43.200
No fare would be foolish
enough to arrive in a cab,

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:46.410
commit the murder, then hop back
in the cab and return home.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:50.830
They would be seen by the driver
as they got in and out, you see.

00:12:50.830 --> 00:12:56.450
Which means that the culprit was someone
who bore absolutely no risk of that happening.

00:12:56.450 --> 00:13:02.250
Which is to say, it was the man
who was driving the cab himself.

00:13:02.250 --> 00:13:07.450
And so, I set the Irregulars off in search
of someone who fit the following criteria:

00:13:07.450 --> 00:13:11.450
A cab driver, who was a large
man with pudgy fingers,

00:13:11.450 --> 00:13:15.330
who also went to Number 3
on the day of the crime.

00:13:15.330 --> 00:13:17.750
One person came up right away.

00:13:17.750 --> 00:13:20.750
You mean, Jefferson Hope.

00:13:21.910 --> 00:13:27.580
Not to mention that Hope's name
is also in Lestrade's report.

00:13:27.580 --> 00:13:33.910
And so, it wasn't very hard
to arrive at you, Hope.

00:13:33.910 --> 00:13:39.580
One question, though. Why did you kill
Drebber in such a coarse manner?

00:13:39.580 --> 00:13:45.950
The plan that was given to you must
have been more careful, and elegant.

00:13:47.910 --> 00:13:51.910
It's true. It was supposed to be
carried out with more finesse.

00:13:51.910 --> 00:13:54.750
But in that moment, when he...

00:13:54.750 --> 00:13:57.830
When Drebber and I came face to face...

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:10.000
...I knew he killed Lucy!

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:14.040
Do you expect me to remain level-headed
when confronting a man like that?!

00:14:15.160 --> 00:14:17.750
Sherlock Holmes...

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:22.250
...would you care to make a deal with me?

00:14:36.700 --> 00:14:41.580
Jefferson Hope, if you've already achieved
your goal, isn't that enough?

00:14:41.580 --> 00:14:43.870
Why don't you quietly turn yourself in?

00:14:43.870 --> 00:14:46.200
Turn myself in?

00:14:52.750 --> 00:15:00.160
As you can see, I do not have much time.
I may as well be dead already.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:04.200
This is why I have given
my life to my benefactor.

00:15:04.200 --> 00:15:07.000
So what are the terms of your deal?

00:15:07.000 --> 00:15:09.910
It is a very simple matter.

00:15:14.870 --> 00:15:18.000
My life has been ruined by
the corruption of aristocrats,

00:15:18.000 --> 00:15:22.040
and I want you to bring an end to my tale.

00:15:25.410 --> 00:15:29.620
Which is to say, I want you to kill me.

00:15:30.620 --> 00:15:33.330
I could ask for nothing more
than to die by your hand,

00:15:33.330 --> 00:15:36.950
after you uncovered Drebber's wrongdoing.

00:15:41.290 --> 00:15:43.120
Who's that?

00:15:43.120 --> 00:15:46.700
The one who got Lucy's ring back for me.

00:15:50.080 --> 00:15:52.330
If you will put an end to my life,

00:15:52.330 --> 00:15:57.410
it has been arranged so that he will
tell you who my benefactor is.

00:15:57.410 --> 00:16:03.450
How thorough of you to consider
what will happen after you're dead.

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:09.080
Yes, indeed. My benefactor truly is amazing.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:12.120
When I arrived at Drebber's mansion
at the appointed time,

00:16:12.120 --> 00:16:17.200
the door opened as if by magic,
and there was not a soul around to interfere.

00:16:17.200 --> 00:16:21.200
A true criminal genius.

00:16:21.200 --> 00:16:24.200
My benefactor holds you in high esteem.

00:16:24.200 --> 00:16:30.040
Enough to leave your name at the scene,
to see if you can solve the case.

00:16:30.040 --> 00:16:34.870
To what end? Why create such an elaborate plan?

00:16:34.870 --> 00:16:40.450
You'll have to ask any further questions
after you've killed me.

00:16:42.660 --> 00:16:48.750
Then again... at the moment,
I'm still a suspect at large.

00:16:48.750 --> 00:16:52.500
If I kill you, I will remain a wanted man.

00:16:52.500 --> 00:16:55.870
Don't worry about that. Once you shoot me,

00:16:55.870 --> 00:17:02.750
he will arrange for a witness to come
forward with some expedient testimony.

00:17:02.750 --> 00:17:06.790
How does that sound?
It's a good arrangement for you, too.

00:17:06.790 --> 00:17:11.540
There is no downside to
this decision for either of us.

00:17:11.540 --> 00:17:14.120
Consider it carefully.

00:17:24.040 --> 00:17:25.790
Indeed...

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.790
That would be a relief.

00:17:31.330 --> 00:17:35.370
Stop, Sherlock! This is wrong!

00:17:36.620 --> 00:17:38.410
Why?

00:17:38.410 --> 00:17:41.700
There is nothing but benefit
for both him and me here.

00:17:41.700 --> 00:17:45.830
Even so, you can't! If you do this,
there'll be no going back!

00:17:45.830 --> 00:17:48.700
Don't listen to him, Holmes!

00:17:48.700 --> 00:17:54.580
You're a man who seeks mysteries.
As such, the answer is a matter of course.

00:17:54.580 --> 00:17:58.370
You want to know who it is that can
create the ultimate mystery, don't you?

00:17:58.370 --> 00:18:02.700
So, shoot me! Do that and you'll--

00:18:07.120 --> 00:18:09.660
Put the gun down, Sherlock!

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:12.330
Don't do it!

00:18:31.120 --> 00:18:33.330
You idiot, of course I'm not going to kill you!

00:18:33.330 --> 00:18:37.450
Wh-Why not? Don't you want
to know about my benefactor?

00:18:37.450 --> 00:18:41.200
You don't understand me <i>at all</i>.

00:18:41.200 --> 00:18:44.410
What do you mean, you'll tell me,
you bloody bastard?

00:18:44.410 --> 00:18:48.620
See here! Mysteries aren't mysteries
unless you solve them yourself!

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:53.700
There's no chance of enjoying them if someone
just gives you the answer, you stupid sod!

00:18:55.910 --> 00:18:57.620
Thank God...

00:18:58.750 --> 00:19:01.580
Seriously, thank God.

00:19:01.580 --> 00:19:05.040
--John?
--Sherlock!

00:19:05.040 --> 00:19:06.120
What's come over you, John?!

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:10.620
Please forgive me for doubting you,
even for a moment!

00:19:10.620 --> 00:19:14.040
All right! All right, just get off of me!

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:16.700
I'm sorry, Hope.

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:20.620
I couldn't grant you your final wish.

00:19:31.910 --> 00:19:37.870
<i>Competent </i>gentlemen of Scotland Yard!
I'd like to introduce someone!

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:43.830
This is the actual killer of Count Enoch
J. Drebber, Jefferson Hope!

00:19:43.830 --> 00:19:46.290
Holmes! Why, you--!

00:19:47.580 --> 00:19:49.200
Holmes!

00:19:51.000 --> 00:19:54.620
I kept my promise, Lestrade.

00:19:57.370 --> 00:20:01.410
Consulting detective Sherlock Holmes...

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:08.540
He slipped away from Scotland Yard
and solved the case, did he?

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:10.290
It's impressive how he discerned

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:13.450
that planner and the executor of the
murder plot were two different people.

00:20:13.450 --> 00:20:17.830
His competency as a detective
appears to be quite genuine.

00:20:17.830 --> 00:20:21.500
This audition has achieved the best
result we could have hoped for.

00:20:21.500 --> 00:20:26.160
It was Holmes's quality that I wished to test.

00:20:26.160 --> 00:20:33.790
That being, whether or not he would resort
to any means to achieve his goal.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:37.160
Even after dangling the promise of
information about us in front of him,

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:40.750
he still didn't go for Hope's proposal.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:45.200
According to Brother's analysis,
he is likely to have a strong interest

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:48.160
in whoever is pulling the strings
behind the scenes.

00:20:48.160 --> 00:20:51.370
And yet, he didn't kill him.

00:20:51.370 --> 00:20:55.950
He's not like us; he won't stop
at nothing to get what he wants.

00:20:55.950 --> 00:21:00.120
Which means that he's worthy, right?

00:21:01.660 --> 00:21:06.870
From here on, he will be taking on
the role of solving the crime drama

00:21:06.870 --> 00:21:10.080
that we will be staging
on the streets of London.

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:16.290
Further, he will be our leading man,

00:21:16.290 --> 00:21:18.790
who shines a light into
the darkness of the world.

00:21:24.620 --> 00:21:27.450
Sherlock Holmes...

00:21:39.080 --> 00:21:41.370
It's morning already?

00:21:44.330 --> 00:21:47.370
I think I'll go downstairs and make some tea.

00:21:55.950 --> 00:21:59.000
As long as I'm living here with you,

00:21:59.000 --> 00:22:04.000
it doesn't seem like I'll ever want
for things to write in my diary.

00:22:07.580 --> 00:22:11.250
<i>An oath committed by souls assumed,</i>

00:22:11.250 --> 00:22:15.450
<i>with ideals that wallow in genetic karma</i>

00:22:29.450 --> 00:22:34.540
<i>Lie! Be resolute if you're going</i>
<i>to share your pain</i>

00:22:34.540 --> 00:22:36.790
<i>(Demonstrate your resolve)</i>

00:22:36.790 --> 00:22:40.290
<i>Like, dislike, you can't afford</i>
<i>to care either way</i>

00:22:40.290 --> 00:22:43.500
<i>It depends on the objective</i>
<i>(Making it happen is what's most important)</i>

00:22:43.500 --> 00:22:47.040
<i>The necessary evil of self-sacrifice</i>

00:22:47.040 --> 00:22:50.660
<i>is a righteous cause that no one can comprehend</i>

00:22:50.660 --> 00:22:54.410
<i>Take pride in the inviolable bonds shared</i>

00:22:54.410 --> 00:22:59.250
<i>among brothers who bear the same crime</i>

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:02.910
<i>Make a chance trigger your inevitable stance</i>

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:04.580
<i>and get drunk on your accomplishments</i>

00:23:04.580 --> 00:23:06.370
<i>at the end</i>

00:23:06.370 --> 00:23:09.540
<i>Don't believe in what you can see,</i>

00:23:09.540 --> 00:23:13.790
<i>in truths there's no point in knowing</i>
<i>(Whatever it takes)</i>

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:20.370
<i>Defer to its own validity,</i>
<i>you only have one goal</i>

00:23:20.370 --> 00:23:21.870
<i>(Let me hear you say)</i>

00:23:24.250 --> 00:23:28.700
<i>You can't lie to yourself, come along with me</i>

00:23:31.660 --> 00:23:34.370
<i>It's all because there is no answer</i>

00:23:34.370 --> 00:23:36.750
<i>There is no turning back</i>

00:23:45.160 --> 00:23:51.950
Next time on <i>Moriarty the Patriot</i>:
"The Two Detectives Act 1."
